{
 "id": "fingerspelling",
 "title": "Fingerspelling, numbers and modifiers",
 "goal": "Spell any word letter by letter, press Ctrl/Alt/Super/Shift shortcuts, digits, function keys, arrows and symbols, and know the rule that generates all 5,475 modifier strokes.",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "t": "h",
   "x": "Two systems"
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "x": "There are two fingerspelling systems, for two different jobs:"
  },
  {
   "t": "table",
   "head": [
    "System",
    "For",
    "Strokes",
    "Where"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "Glued letters",
     "spelling a name, acronym or any word the dictionary lacks, in the text",
     "a letter chord plus `*` (52 strokes)",
     "`rules/reserved/punctuation.json`"
    ],
    [
     "Modifier chords ('Emily's' dictionary)",
     "OS key presses: Ctrl+C, Alt+Tab, Shift+B, F-keys, arrows, media keys",
     "a chord plus modifier keys plus the ender `LGTS` (5,475 strokes)",
     "`rules/reserved/emilys-modifiers.py` and `.json`"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "x": "Both are reserved ahead of every word ([reserved strokes](#/ref/reserved-strokes)). The modifier dictionary is forked from Lapwing's \"Emily's Modifier Dictionary\"; it is plain Plover fingerspelling plus a chorded Ctrl/Alt/Super/Shift ending, independent of the theory, and works the same under lyk."
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "x": "The alphabet"
  },
  {
